# Line endings are decided here, not per machine.
#
# This repo is checked out on Linux and on Windows (hermine), where the bash
# scripts additionally run under WSL against the same working tree. CRLF in a
# file that bash reads is a hard failure, not cosmetic:
#   .bashrc-functions  -> "Syntaxfehler beim unerwarteten Wort $'{\r'"
#   config.env.example -> "$'\r': Befehl nicht gefunden" on every line
#   *.sh               -> "set: pipefail: invalid option name"
# core.autocrlf cannot express this: it is per-clone and not committed, which
# is how CRLF got into .bashrc-aliases/.bashrc-functions in the first place.
#
# Gitignored files are NOT covered (config.env, config.ps1, presets/models.ini),
# so the CR stripping in config-load.sh and recommend.sh stays load-bearing.

# Default: LF in the index and in every working tree, on every platform.
*        text=auto eol=lf

# Exception: PowerShell entry points are checked out CRLF on Windows.
# The index still stores LF, so cross-machine diffs stay clean.
# Note this does not match config.ps1.example, which bash reads and which
# therefore stays LF.
*.ps1    text eol=crlf
